control 'CKV_AWS_277' do
  title 'Ensure no security groups allow ingress from 0.0.0.0:0 to port -1'

  desc <<~DESC
    Checkov asserts this against Terraform. This profile asserts it against
    the aws_security_group resources that actually exist, enumerated through
    the declarative API spec.

    Deployed rather than declared, so a permission opened by hand is seen.
    It also reads the protocol AWS actually stored rather than the
    from_port/to_port pair the HCL declared, which is the only form in which
    an all-traffic rule exists after apply.
  DESC

  desc 'rationale', <<~RATIONALE
    An all-protocols, all-ports ingress permission open to 0.0.0.0/0 removes
    the security group as a boundary entirely; every service the instance
    happens to be listening on, including ones nobody knows are listening,
    is reachable from the internet. It is also the rule that survives
    longest, because it is usually written to unblock something during an
    incident and never narrowed afterwards, and nothing about the workload
    breaks to remind anyone.
  RATIONALE

  desc 'check', <<~CHECK
    Checkov looks for: aws_security_group: an INGRESS permission with
    IpProtocol "-1" — every protocol and every port — whose IpRanges contain
    0.0.0.0/0 or whose Ipv6Ranges contain ::/0.
  CHECK

  desc 'fix', <<~'FIX'
    Terraform — aws_security_group:

      resource "aws_security_group" "app" {
        name   = "app"
        vpc_id = aws_vpc.example.id

        # No protocol = "-1" ingress block, and no cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"].
        # Every port that must be reachable is named, with the source that is
        # allowed to reach it.
        ingress {
          description     = "app traffic from the ALB"
          from_port       = 8443
          to_port         = 8443
          protocol        = "tcp"
          security_groups = [aws_security_group.alb.id]
        }
      }

    Out of band — aws_security_group:

      aws ec2 revoke-security-group-ingress --group-id <sg-id> --ip-permissions IpProtocol=-1,IpRanges=[{CidrIp=0.0.0.0/0}]
      aws ec2 revoke-security-group-ingress --group-id <sg-id> --ip-permissions IpProtocol=-1,Ipv6Ranges=[{CidrIpv6=::/0}]

    Note (aws_security_group): An IpProtocol of "-1" is revoked as a protocol,
    not as a port range — there are no FromPort or ToPort members on the stored
    permission to name. Revoke it before writing the replacement rules if the
    exposure is live; otherwise write the narrow rules first so nothing is
    interrupted, then revoke. Confirm what was actually reaching the instance
    (VPC flow logs, or the load balancer's target health) before assuming the
    narrow set is complete.
